<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #eaf2f8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # It's the Weekend. – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> All Week Long!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's the Weekend.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Recognize familiar words and basic phrases. Interactive: Respond to simple conversations on familiar topics by using gestures or by pointing.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can recognize familiar words related to days of the week spoken slowly (e.g., "Monday." "Today is Monday." "It's Monday."). Can respond to the basic days of the week and weekend by pointing at the word or position on a basic calendar. Can recognize and respond to questions about the days of the week using basic phrases (e.g., "Is today Tuesday?" "No, it is Saturday." "It is the weekend.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 25px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on engaging students, activating prior knowledge, modeling new language, and clarifying concepts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Days of the Week Song" with Total Physical Response (TPR). The teacher sings a simple song (e.g., to the tune of "Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star") like "Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, day. Thursday, Friday, Saturday, day. Sunday is the weekend day! Fun, fun, fun all the way!" Students listen and clap or do simple actions like pointing up for "day" or making a relaxed gesture for "weekend". The teacher holds up large, colorful flashcards for each day of the week as they are sung.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary (Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday, weekend, day, week) using large flashcards. The teacher says each word clearly and slowly, focusing on /w/ in "weekend" and /d/ in "day." Students listen and repeat chorally. The teacher also models simple phrases like "Today is Monday." and "It is the weekend." using gestures (pointing to the calendar for "today," making a relaxing gesture for "weekend").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the concept of "day" and "weekend."</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to "Saturday" and "Sunday" flashcards: "Is this the weekend?" (Expected response: "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to "Monday" flashcard: "Is this the weekend?" (Expected response: "No!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says: "Today is Monday." The teacher points to a Monday flashcard. "Is today Monday?" (Expected response: "Yes!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage introduces new language content connected to the scenario and theme through authentic input.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario "All Week Long!":</b> The teacher uses a large, colorful weekly schedule chart or a simple calendar displaying the days of the week with pictures of activities (e.g., school for weekdays, playing for weekend). The teacher points to each day, says its name, and a simple phrase related to it. For example, "Look! Monday. Today is Monday. School day!" (pointing to a school picture) and "Look! Saturday. It is the weekend. Relaxing!" (pointing to a child playing).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated weekly schedule chart / basic calendar showing days of the week, flashcards of days and simple corresponding activities (e.g., school, park, relaxing at home).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic Competence: Using time-related vocabulary: Identifying words for days and weekends by using or pointing to them. Sociolinguistic Competence: Classroom schedules: Using polite expressions when talking about the days of the week and routines.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Listening):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> The teacher asks: "What do you hear about today?" (Expected response: "Monday!" or "School!"). The teacher asks: "What do you hear about Saturday?" (Expected response: "Weekend!" or "Play!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching (Pointing):</b> The teacher says a day (e.g., "Listen: Friday."). Students point to the "Friday" on the large chart. The teacher says "Weekend!" Students point to Saturday or Sunday.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on accuracy with controlled and semi-controlled practice of the target language, specifically focusing on listening skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening: Recognize familiar words and basic phrases related to days of the week. Grammar: Noun + verb + noun (e.g., "Today is Monday."), Present simple (e.g., "It is the weekend."). Vocabulary: Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday, weekend, day.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Listen and Point:</b> Students receive a worksheet with small pictures of the days of the week (flashcards in a grid). The teacher says a day or phrase (e.g., "Monday.", "Today is Tuesday.", "It is the weekend."). Students listen and point to the corresponding picture/word on their worksheet. The teacher repeats each word/phrase 2-3 times slowly and clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Listen and Respond (TPR):</b> The teacher says simple "Yes/No" questions. Students listen and respond using gestures (nod for "Yes," shake head for "No"). Examples: "Is today Wednesday?" "Is Saturday the weekend?" "Is Friday busy?" (Using adjective vocabulary). The teacher encourages 1st graders to also try to say "Yes" or "No."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Listen and Match):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair has a set of small flashcards for the days of the week. One student (Student A) closes their eyes. The other student (Student B) says a day (e.g., "Listen: Sunday!"). Student A opens their eyes and points to the "Sunday" card. They switch roles. The teacher monitors closely, modeling correct pronunciation as needed.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 15 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage involves students using the target language in a more realistic and communicative task within the given scenario and theme.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task: "What Day Is Today?" Circle Game.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students sit in a large circle. The teacher holds a large flashcard of "today's day" (e.g., "Tuesday"). The teacher starts by asking the student next to them, "What day is today?" The teacher models the answer: "Today is Tuesday." The student then turns to the next student and asks the same question, "What day is today?" The teacher ensures the student listens to the question and then points to the correct day flashcard if available, or just repeats the answer. If a student struggles to respond, the teacher prompts, "Listen. Today is..." or points to the flashcard. This focuses on receptive listening to the question and simple recognition/pointing for response. The teacher can introduce "It is the weekend" for Saturday/Sunday if appropriate for the actual day of the lesson.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Group interaction, simple question-and-answer exchange (listening for the question, simple oral or pointing response).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme "It's the Weekend." and Scenario "All Week Long!":</b> Students are actively using vocabulary for days of the week, acknowledging "today," and recognizing if it's a weekday or weekend. The teacher will swap in "Saturday" or "Sunday" flashcards if the lesson falls on a weekend day, leading to the response "It is the weekend."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 8 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on formative assessment and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher observes students' ability to recognize spoken days of the week and respond to simple questions by pointing or using gestures. The teacher notes down which students accurately identify the days after listening.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: Listen and Choose.</b> The teacher holds up two day-of-the-week flashcards (e.g., "Monday" and "Friday"). The teacher says one of the days (e.g., "Point to Monday!"). Students listen and physically point to the correct flashcard. This is repeated for several pairs of days, including "weekend" vs. "weekday." The teacher also asks, "Is today [day]?" and students respond with "Yes" or "No" (or a head nod/shake).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Listening Focus):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in pointing to the correct day of the week when heard (recognize familiar words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to respond to "Yes/No" questions about days of the week using gestures or simple verbal cues (respond to simple conversations).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognition of "weekend" when spoken.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to the day you heard most today."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Show me a 'weekend' picture if you know it."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 7 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This final stage encourages both student and teacher reflection on the learning process and outlines connections to future lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What word did you listen to well today?" (Encourage pointing to a flashcard or drawing a simple representation).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is your favorite day to hear?" (Students can point to the day).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening lesson? (e.g., TPR, flashcards, repetition, small group activity for pointing).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students struggling with listening comprehension of the days of the week? (e.g., more one-on-one pronunciation practice, louder volume, slower pace, simpler choices for pointing). Were the CCQs effective?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully recognize the /w/ and /d/ sounds in "weekend" and "day"?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will engage with a short text or simple labels that include these same days of the week and "weekend" vocabulary. They will listen to the teacher read the text aloud, then recognize and match the written words of the days of the week with corresponding pictures, building on their listening recognition by connecting it to visual word forms.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 5 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>